---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PythagoreanTheorem from '@/components/tools/calc2/PythagoreanTheorem.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pythagorean-theorem-calculator">
  <PythagoreanTheorem client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the Pythagorean theorem calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      In a right triangle, the square on the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) has the same
      area as the squares on the other two sides together. The Pythagorean theorem calculator uses this
      to find the missing side from the other two.
    </p>
    <pre><code>a² + b² = c²
c = √(a² + b²)        hypotenuse from the legs
a = √(c² − b²)        a leg from the hypotenuse and the other leg</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      The legs are 3 and 4. Then c² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so c = √25 = 5. The animation shows the
      9 and 16 unit squares filling the 25 unit square on the hypotenuse.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find a leg: a ladder 13 ft long stands 5 ft from a wall. The height it reaches is
      √(13² − 5²) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12 ft.
    </p>

    <h2>Pythagorean triples</h2>
    <p>
      Whole-number sides that fit the theorem are Pythagorean triples: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17, 7-24-25
      and their multiples, such as 6-8-10. Builders use 3-4-5 to set out a square corner.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The theorem holds only for a right triangle. For other triangles, use the law of cosines.</li>
      <li>The hypotenuse is always the longest side. A leg longer than or equal to the hypotenuse gives no triangle.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
